@h1 Defining nodes h1@

A definition creates a reusable template: @@@code #name … name# code@@@ defines
it, and @@@code @name code@@@ uses it. Definitions are the core of reuse —
cards, citations, chapter wrappers, callouts. This page covers the three body
shapes, capture lists, the @@@code ::name:: code@@@ hole, and the @@@code ...
code@@@ body slot. Read it before Interpolation & captures and before Additive
partials.

@h2 Three body shapes h2@

@ul
@li @strong Block strong@ — @@@code #name … name# code@@@ wraps multiple lines
. li@
@li @strong Single-line strong@ — @@@code #name: value !! code@@@ names a short
value; the @@@code !! code@@@ closes it (single-line-def,
single-line-def-with-captures). li@
@li @strong Value-block strong@ — @@@code #name: code@@@ then indented content
then @@@code !! code@@@ names a longer value that may span lines (multi-line-value). li@
ul@

@@@pre
#epigraph:
The sea does not forgive forgetting.
— coastal proverb
!!

Attention begins here. @epigraph
pre@@@

@h2 Capture lists: explicit or inferred h2@

A capture list names the parameters a template accepts, written
@@@code ||a, b, c|| code@@@ right after the handle. Inside the body, each
@@@code ::name:: code@@@ is filled by the matching parameter.

@@@pre
#cite ||author, title, year||
::author:: (::year::). _::title::_.
cite#
pre@@@

 The
list is @strong optional strong@: leave it out and Wit infers the captures by
scanning the body for @@@code ::ident:: code@@@ in source order.

@@@pre
#tpl hello ::name:: tpl#

@tpl |name world|
pre@@@

That renders @code hello world code@. Inference walks into nested
paragraphs, emphasis, node uses, and @@@code (if) code@@@ / @@@code (each) code@@@
bodies, so a hole anywhere in the template is found.

@h2 The capture hole and the body slot h2@

@@@code ::name:: code@@@ is recognised @strong only inside a definition body
strong@. In
ordinary prose it renders literally — to put data into a sentence, use data
access @@@code @name.field code@@@ instead.

The @strong body slot strong@ @@@code ... code@@@ splices the use-site body into
the template once:

@@@pre
#panel
START ... END
panel#

@panel
middle
panel@
pre@@@

That yields @code START code@, then @code middle code@, then @code END code@
.

@h2 Forward references and defs that use defs h2@

Definitions are @strong hoisted strong@, so a use may precede its definition —
@@@code @x code@@@ before @@@code #x code@@@ resolves fine. A definition body may reference another definition:

@@@pre
#keeper Ada Lovelace keeper#

#bio Written by @keeper. bio#
pre@@@



@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li Using @@@code ::name:: code@@@ in prose — it only lives in a definition
body. li@
@li Redefining the same @@@code #name code@@@ twice — an error, unless you mark
it additive with @@@code +#name code@@@ (see Additive partials). li@
@li Expecting a use-site parameter to reach an inner @@@code (if) code@@@ unless the
template surfaces it through a capture. li@
ul@

